ext "step-6da7d17f-49ff-520c-9d2b-7b16e9928b0c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6da7d17f-49ff-520c-9d2b-7b16e9928b0c" :step/id #uuid "6da7d17f-49ff-520c-9d2b-7b16e9928b0c" :step/content "question-c20addaa-2ef3-5691-8ace-1f1d2895fe58" :step/next "step-5ed022d1-55d7-5557-9818-d5cd788aef99" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5ed022d1-55d7-5557-9818-d5cd788aef99" :step/id #uuid "5ed022d1-55d7-5557-9818-d5cd788aef99" :step/content "question-2c796978-9336-5fc7-a4cc-bd1fe1ccd4cd" :step/next "step-8910007d-04c4-5e67-88ba-19f39a138b47" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8910007d-04c4-5e67-88ba-19f39a138b47" :step/id #uuid "8910007d-04c4-5e67-88ba-19f39a138b47" :step/content "question-d9b9c93d-9a95-57ab-bfc8-b62b1c153ff8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100946 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9683153e-7324-5fb1-80ce-172326e0407d" "question-311d3c6d-fd7c-5156-9816-24d4c0a4cb73"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9683153e-7324-5fb1-80ce-172326e0407d" :question/id #uuid "9683153e-7324-5fb1-80ce-172326e0407d" :question/math-academy-id "q-87243" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa8eef8fc185109d90dcc6c17f9684db06050d9b2cf63560186c45e8ce0ac204.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(3(x+2))?$" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(3(x+2)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(3)}=\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x).$\n\n![](images/30/3027f0943e238d63996c8d1ff36d36b14bb60c18957168f7e54bb0a7ddcd9d33.png)\n\n  \n\n- Then, translate it left by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f(3(x+2)).$\n\n![](images/e3/e3e552a5bacd539ee409a69be7e69cddc4e4e2404bcc4adc5173e980e8c8ca93.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b75424f3-f13f-5b3f-a284-ab8eea9d099c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b75424f3-f13f-5b3f-a284-ab8eea9d099c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b75424f3-f13f-5b3f-a284-ab8eea9d099c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f5e48cb0-af41-59b9-8d6c-47bcc06eafa9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-40e5f0af-4b75-5a30-a17d-4417edb56708" "answer-d376679e-80e4-5df5-aa72-5c8effe9d4b7" "answer-bcef57bc-48c4-5725-b238-e1b4e144afb3" "answer-f5e48cb0-af41-59b9-8d6c-47bcc06eafa9" "answer-b7b9f146-90ab-5464-a6ea-34df9c5dba5b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40e5f0af-4b75-5a30-a17d-4417edb56708" :answer/id #uuid "40e5f0af-4b75-5a30-a17d-4417edb56708" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/34/343546800fc6ecd2945048933101bbbc19a73d4ac7e6a676a2cf46f9be70adf8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d376679e-80e4-5df5-aa72-5c8effe9d4b7" :answer/id #uuid "d376679e-80e4-5df5-aa72-5c8effe9d4b7" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/62/624f7a53993bd26396aaa597669f2c3f44a03baf92e5c5da87a7f17ea5a83d4a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcef57bc-48c4-5725-b238-e1b4e144afb3" :answer/id #uuid "bcef57bc-48c4-5725-b238-e1b4e144afb3" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/6c/6cf121bb9b724d0367eab935b03dd51f46fa0c279906c06a9bdfc96e6c79f369.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5e48cb0-af41-59b9-8d6c-47bcc06eafa9" :answer/id #uuid "f5e48cb0-af41-59b9-8d6c-47bcc06eafa9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/77/77c4c913c552c4560b4f22a6ac07726adb6a4d99450db3862412ea96a11f1d2a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7b9f146-90ab-5464-a6ea-34df9c5dba5b" :answer/id #uuid "b7b9f146-90ab-5464-a6ea-34df9c5dba5b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/fa/fa8eef8fc185109d90dcc6c17f9684db06050d9b2cf63560186c45e8ce0ac204.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101561 :question/problem "![](images/64/642590d3e04de8365075f79f7954c5123019326b1f4addc618a9a2f54a09e881.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(x-1)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-1)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$\n\n![](images/da/da5e6848776232c4f4429c8b3c5e3fdaaae2e9a99a180dfe409893e97d407036.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(x-1)+3.$\n\n![](images/81/8188a686d0f9c579f44601645667c0209a39b6500864eb9479147940ef1ebb26.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100527 :answer-field/correct 17592186100531 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-311d3c6d-fd7c-5156-9816-24d4c0a4cb73" :question/id #uuid "311d3c6d-fd7c-5156-9816-24d4c0a4cb73" :question/math-academy-id "q-112634" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec193bb78fbfb43cec5266622ef0bef14e6aad5798687ae9c7cc7233e69078cb.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(3(x-5))?$" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(3(x-5)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(3)}=\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x).$\n\n![](images/c4/c41d06f741882e3993ebe8eb7eab5c42c362d3c11b9b8ad1e4fed983498c95d0.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $5$ units to get the graph of $y=f(3(x-5)).$\n\n![](images/47/47bf38d228b4337b870baf777c53fbab43743f155f4a23aa6d0e2bd1f5bc45ad.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fa09890c-3a44-50c2-b038-e436dbd0b29c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa09890c-3a44-50c2-b038-e436dbd0b29c" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa09890c-3a44-50c2-b038-e436dbd0b29c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2063484c-96a8-5c06-bd28-f076ec33f08c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2063484c-96a8-5c06-bd28-f076ec33f08c" "answer-d7d03033-d79c-5acc-9d9a-2046cbf19feb" "answer-fd9599ea-f6e5-5373-b3ba-146eb8b30782" "answer-8cbcc19b-28b6-5bd8-8f5c-4c8722e65c0d" "answer-03bfc5d5-8208-5f39-8039-f35d61e04db4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2063484c-96a8-5c06-bd28-f076ec33f08c" :answer/id #uuid "2063484c-96a8-5c06-bd28-f076ec33f08c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1a/1a832c774aeb07399060817149462c2e11c3313d5ad14816a8b6092abfef702b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7d03033-d79c-5acc-9d9a-2046cbf19feb" :answer/id #uuid "d7d03033-d79c-5acc-9d9a-2046cbf19feb" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1e/1ee5948af075803bbab3e22ce9d22532cd8aa4e73679c01a93205e45311aa25c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd9599ea-f6e5-5373-b3ba-146eb8b30782" :answer/id #uuid "fd9599ea-f6e5-5373-b3ba-146eb8b30782" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a4/a4dd99ebd96ecf960998264b8defb7b9f27bd1c411aa2cea6d257c5182ac7fc1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cbcc19b-28b6-5bd8-8f5c-4c8722e65c0d" :answer/id #uuid "8cbcc19b-28b6-5bd8-8f5c-4c8722e65c0d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a9/a917739154143a16c1c5d76948d0392ce7376be034a9ca6e162cfd829594c2a7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03bfc5d5-8208-5f39-8039-f35d61e04db4" :answer/id #uuid "03bfc5d5-8208-5f39-8039-f35d61e04db4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/df/dfe8088aa2f2b09841223574c813c9197c29134eb9503e1697e852cd7184514f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-d24b7158-f960-57fe-bd31-a6935529e708" :activity/id #uuid "d24b7158-f960-57fe-bd31-a6935529e708" :activity/title "Combining Graph Transformations: Two Operations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050085 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-0b8293c3-52d1-5621-abf8-52fde1e15572" "step-7d9227a6-c2b7-5b74-9e9f-8551f850121c" "step-f7d4e6ad-eb99-5709-b8bf-9064ece78df6"] :activity/first-step "step-0b8293c3-52d1-5621-abf8-52fde1e15572" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-0b8293c3-52d1-5621-abf8-52fde1e15572" :step/id #uuid "0b8293c3-52d1-5621-abf8-52fde1e15572" :step/content "question-9683153e-7324-5fb1-80ce-172326e0407d" :step/next "step-7d9227a6-c2b7-5b74-9e9f-8551f850121c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7d9227a6-c2b7-5b74-9e9f-8551f850121c" :step/id #uuid "7d9227a6-c2b7-5b74-9e9f-8551f850121c" :step/content 17592186101561 :step/next "step-f7d4e6ad-eb99-5709-b8bf-9064ece78df6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f7d4e6ad-eb99-5709-b8bf-9064ece78df6" :step/id #uuid "f7d4e6ad-eb99-5709-b8bf-9064ece78df6" :step/content "question-311d3c6d-fd7c-5156-9816-24d4c0a4cb73" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124542 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-15721919-affc-527a-806f-d54311da77ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-15721919-affc-527a-806f-d54311da77ac" :question/id #uuid "15721919-affc-527a-806f-d54311da77ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-97580" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-daee834d-972a-5370-8934-102b16e2168b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-daee834d-972a-5370-8934-102b16e2168b" :answer-field/id #uuid "daee834d-972a-5370-8934-102b16e2168b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-209da59d-2b86-550d-89c6-5385f915b5e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15d13c75-e6bc-5e2e-a8a5-6dd560e659ae" "answer-d70dba8c-62c5-5199-93bd-0c9dd94ff141" "answer-bfe4a7d2-be73-53f9-b806-31ea43cf2ba9" "answer-209da59d-2b86-550d-89c6-5385f915b5e2" "answer-570944f0-ed2d-54ac-afa7-c23ef2d1f070"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15d13c75-e6bc-5e2e-a8a5-6dd560e659ae" :answer/id #uuid "15d13c75-e6bc-5e2e-a8a5-6dd560e659ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d70dba8c-62c5-5199-93bd-0c9dd94ff141" :answer/id #uuid "d70dba8c-62c5-5199-93bd-0c9dd94ff141" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfe4a7d2-be73-53f9-b806-31ea43cf2ba9" :answer/id #uuid "bfe4a7d2-be73-53f9-b806-31ea43cf2ba9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-209da59d-2b86-550d-89c6-5385f915b5e2" :answer/id #uuid "209da59d-2b86-550d-89c6-5385f915b5e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-570944f0-ed2d-54ac-afa7-c23ef2d1f070" :answer/id #uuid "570944f0-ed2d-54ac-afa7-c23ef2d1f070" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119844 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fc096f25-da43-5ef0-8dca-9310fea097c1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fc096f25-da43-5ef0-8dca-9310fea097c1" :question/id #uuid "fc096f25-da43-5ef0-8dca-9310fea097c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-42981" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 8 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8429c5f3-10e5-5e89-82ae-2a624d986bbe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8429c5f3-10e5-5e89-82ae-2a624d986bbe" :answer-field/id #uuid "8429c5f3-10e5-5e89-82ae-2a624d986bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-752e27ae-6bc8-5acc-bb2a-b47134fc80d9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8a477f06-b877-5fa0-bce3-9f5744ae6971" "answer-41433e06-ff80-564d-9ce3-d0dc9852a8ee" "answer-249e501c-2f96-5d3a-aca1-a06ed673e1ac" "answer-752e27ae-6bc8-5acc-bb2a-b47134fc80d9" "answer-2592d63c-e1be-5dbf-bf09-9ff0d9f2f561"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a477f06-b877-5fa0-bce3-9f5744ae6971" :answer/id #uuid "8a477f06-b877-5fa0-bce3-9f5744ae6971" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & \\,-28 \\\\ 2 & \\,11\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41433e06-ff80-564d-9ce3-d0dc9852a8ee" :answer/id #uuid "41433e06-ff80-564d-9ce3-d0dc9852a8ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-249e501c-2f96-5d3a-aca1-a06ed673e1ac" :answer/id #uuid "249e501c-2f96-5d3a-aca1-a06ed673e1ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 5 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-752e27ae-6bc8-5acc-bb2a-b47134fc80d9" :answer/id #uuid "752e27ae-6bc8-5acc-bb2a-b47134fc80d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 8 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2592d63c-e1be-5dbf-bf09-9ff0d9f2f561" :answer/id #uuid "2592d63c-e1be-5dbf-bf09-9ff0d9f2f561" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}\\,11 & 8 \\\\ \\,-28 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192676 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2595de8f-014d-5db6-a5d3-e22363b7564e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2595de8f-014d-5db6-a5d3-e22363b7564e" :question/id #uuid "2595de8f-014d-5db6-a5d3-e22363b7564e" :question/math-academy-id "q-152903" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[9X+4].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{2},\\,0<x<2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[9X+4]=9\\text{E}[X]+4\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[9X+4] & =9\\text{E}[X]+4 \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+4 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d5917ecc-743e-5b98-928c-b4e265150550"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d5917ecc-743e-5b98-928c-b4e265150550" :answer-field/id #uuid "d5917ecc-743e-5b98-928c-b4e265150550" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a24492a0-f30c-5760-a699-262c2d5c1e9b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-00390579-73db-58de-8e3f-f3ca75d10676" "answer-a24492a0-f30c-5760-a699-262c2d5c1e9b" "answer-76eeaeb4-2ab6-520a-b6b0-9a2f15095f14" "answer-6ca1573d-75dd-57d1-975d-20b4eabf81d0" "answer-b388bc7a-f341-596b-93dd-60183e4ec2b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00390579-73db-58de-8e3f-f3ca75d10676" :answer/id #uuid "00390579-73db-58de-8e3f-f3ca75d10676" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a24492a0-f30c-5760-a699-262c2d5c1e9b" :answer/id #uuid "a24492a0-f30c-5760-a699-262c2d5c1e9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76eeaeb4-2ab6-520a-b6b0-9a2f15095f14" :answer/id #uuid "76eeaeb4-2ab6-520a-b6b0-9a2f15095f14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ca1573d-75dd-57d1-975d-20b4eabf81d0" :answer/id #uuid "6ca1573d-75dd-57d1-975d-20b4eabf81d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b388bc7a-f341-596b-93dd-60183e4ec2b1" :answer/id #uuid "b388bc7a-f341-596b-93dd-60183e4ec2b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-be2f0019-a161-5405-a7a2-e77856e00af4" :activity/id #uuid "be2f0019-a161-5405-a7a2-e77856e00af4" :activity/title "Quiz 4" :activity/type :activity.type/assessment :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-580422bf-3d6c-5210-b285-aa8671ab0768" "step-24101b53-04f5-5a42-abcd-bf23100ae66d" "step-d7d92401-cb3f-5281-8499-b4adbba3d575" "step-916157f9-0c04-5b62-b14f-2a351f6e1133" "step-7b187b67-e213-5cbc-9db5-c90f4b06f5c8" "step-37f56bed-06b2-5321-bd27-98c4d186cd0e"] :activity/first-step "step-580422bf-3d6c-5210-b285-aa8671ab0768" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-580422bf-3d6c-5210-b285-aa8671ab0768" :step/id #uuid "580422bf-3d6c-5210-b285-aa8671ab0768" :step/content 17592186266150 :step/next "step-24101b53-04f5-5a42-abcd-bf23100ae66d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-24101b53-04f5-5a42-abcd-bf23100ae66d" :step/id #uuid "24101b53-04f5-5a42-abcd-bf23100ae66d" :step/content 17592186088239 :step/next "step-d7d92401-cb3f-5281-8499-b4adbba3d575" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d7d92401-cb3f-5281-8499-b4adbba3d575" :step/id #uuid "d7d92401-cb3f-5281-8499-b4adbba3d575" :step/content "question-15721919-affc-527a-806f-d54311da77ac" :step/next "step-916157f9-0c04-5b62-b14f-2a351f6e1133" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-916157f9-0c04-5b62-b14f-2a351f6e1133" :step/id #uuid "916157f9-0c04-5b62-b14f-2a351f6e1133" :step/content 17592186129603 :step/next "step-7b187b67-e213-5cbc-9db5-c90f4b06f5c8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7b187b67-e213-5cbc-9db5-c90f4b06f5c8" :step/id #uuid "7b187b67-e213-5cbc-9db5-c90f4b06f5c8" :step/content "question-fc096f25-da43-5ef0-8dca-9310fea097c1" :step/next "step-37f56bed-06b2-5321-bd27-98c4d186cd0e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-37f56bed-06b2-5321-bd27-98c4d186cd0e" :step/id #uuid "37f56bed-06b2-5321-bd27-98c4d186cd0e" :step/content "question-2595de8f-014d-5db6-a5d3-e22363b7564e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078558 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f438fee9-fd2e-5bc4-8ed6-aceb6e4fcc33" "question-91775cc6-b3ff-5403-8a6d-5a5294e1af4c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078202 :question/problem "The first term of a geometric sequence is $2$, and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the $9$th term?" :question/worked-solution "The first term is $a_1=2,$ and the common ratio is $r=3,$ so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $9$th term, we can use this formula with $n={\\color{blue}9}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{9} & =2\\cdot {3}^{9-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{8} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6561 \\\\  & =13\\,122\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is $a_{9}=13\\,122.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078559 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1bbae00a-a76f-5b1d-a767-0acf7498bde8" "question-18bc1e21-0c4b-52f3-824f-e719cd86e034"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078204 :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of a sequence if the $6$th term is $80$ and the common ratio is $-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=-2,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term $a_1.$ We know that the $6$th term is $a_6=80,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{6-1} \\\\ 80 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{5} \\\\ 80 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-32) \\\\ -\\frac{80}{32} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is $a_1=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify a bit further by manipulating the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{5}{2}(-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)^{-1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078560 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cb4d13f6-05f0-58f1-8216-e5e0e82521cb" "question-b5f78cff-3c2d-5ff5-b715-c8fb25e53460"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078203 :question/problem "Given that the $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $9$, and the common ratio is $3,$ find the second term." :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=3,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term $a_1.$ We know that the $5$th term is $a_5=9,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5-1} \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{4} \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot 81 \\\\ \\frac{9}{81} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9} & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is $a_1=\\dfrac{1}{9},$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {3}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the second term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {3}^{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second term is $a_2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f438fee9-fd2e-5bc4-8ed6-aceb6e4fcc33" :question/id #uuid "f438fee9-fd2e-5bc4-8ed6-aceb6e4fcc33" :question/math-academy-id "q-265738" :question/problem "The first term of a geometric sequence is $32,$ and the common ratio is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$  The $12$th term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The first term is ${a}_{1}=32,$ and the common ratio is $r=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $12$th term, we can use this formula with $n=12:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{12} & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{12-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{11} \\\\  & =-{2}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{11}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{2}^{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is ${a}_{12}=-\\frac{1}{64}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-26653816-dfb8-59e5-8d87-31577cdbb120"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-26653816-dfb8-59e5-8d87-31577cdbb120" :answer-field/id #uuid "26653816-dfb8-59e5-8d87-31577cdbb120" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1dd5ec84-7a08-5f98-8682-c2c50317fdda" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1dd5ec84-7a08-5f98-8682-c2c50317fdda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1dd5ec84-7a08-5f98-8682-c2c50317fdda" :answer/id #uuid "1dd5ec84-7a08-5f98-8682-c2c50317fdda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{64}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-91775cc6-b3ff-5403-8a6d-5a5294e1af4c" :question/id #uuid "91775cc6-b3ff-5403-8a6d-5a5294e1af4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-265737" :question/problem "The first term of a geometric sequence is $-\\frac{1}{81},$ and the common ratio is $-3.$ The $9$th term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The first term is ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{81},$ and the common ratio is $r=-3,$ so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $9$th term, we can use this formula with $n=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{9} & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{9-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}}\\cdot {3}^{8} \\\\  & =-{3}^{4} \\\\  & =-81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is ${a}_{9}=-81.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b77092c7-1268-5f33-a5db-81532ae19c78"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b77092c7-1268-5f33-a5db-81532ae19c78" :answer-field/id #uuid "b77092c7-1268-5f33-a5db-81532ae19c78" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-910334bb-a236-530a-b15b-4e9e5b6ad92e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-910334bb-a236-530a-b15b-4e9e5b6ad92e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-910334bb-a236-530a-b15b-4e9e5b6ad92e" :answer/id #uuid "910334bb-a236-530a-b15b-4e9e5b6ad92e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-81" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1bbae00a-a76f-5b1d-a767-0acf7498bde8" :question/id #uuid "1bbae00a-a76f-5b1d-a767-0acf7498bde8" :question/math-academy-id "q-5449" :question/problem "Find the equation for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence if the $5$th term is $16$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{2}{3},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=16,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-1} \\\\ 16 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4} \\\\ {2}^{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{4}}{{3}^{4}}) \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{4}\\cdot {3}^{4}}{{2}^{4}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {3}^{4} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 81 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=81,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =81\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-93f0a508-6eba-52dd-97c6-cb5ace58658c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-93f0a508-6eba-52dd-97c6-cb5ace58658c" :answer-field/id #uuid "93f0a508-6eba-52dd-97c6-cb5ace58658c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eca10867-76a4-52f1-be58-955b24419718" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0aa368f5-9980-5af5-a908-3b9b819b0121" "answer-a7cb3144-a22f-5f9b-a7a4-6681a52052c2" "answer-a8bc2a3c-15ca-50bd-b78f-91ffcfb5a2d5" "answer-cc516a3f-e478-5de4-865d-84d26bdbc656" "answer-eca10867-76a4-52f1-be58-955b24419718"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0aa368f5-9980-5af5-a908-3b9b819b0121" :answer/id #uuid "0aa368f5-9980-5af5-a908-3b9b819b0121" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=100\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7cb3144-a22f-5f9b-a7a4-6681a52052c2" :answer/id #uuid "a7cb3144-a22f-5f9b-a7a4-6681a52052c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=16\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8bc2a3c-15ca-50bd-b78f-91ffcfb5a2d5" :answer/id #uuid "a8bc2a3c-15ca-50bd-b78f-91ffcfb5a2d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=49\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc516a3f-e478-5de4-865d-84d26bdbc656" :answer/id #uuid "cc516a3f-e478-5de4-865d-84d26bdbc656" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=64\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eca10867-76a4-52f1-be58-955b24419718" :answer/id #uuid "eca10867-76a4-52f1-be58-955b24419718" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=81\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-18bc1e21-0c4b-52f3-824f-e719cd86e034" :question/id #uuid "18bc1e21-0c4b-52f3-824f-e719cd86e034" :question/math-academy-id "q-5450" :question/problem "Find the equation for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence if the $6$th term is $-\\frac{1}{5}$ and the common ratio is $-\\frac{1}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=-\\frac{1}{5},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $6$th term is ${a}_{6}=-\\frac{1}{5},$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{6-1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{5} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & =-{a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{5} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & =-{a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{5}^{5}}) \\\\ {5}^{4} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 625 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=625,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =625\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d6e6324-1bb7-5c0e-a897-1048511dc113"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d6e6324-1bb7-5c0e-a897-1048511dc113" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d6e6324-1bb7-5c0e-a897-1048511dc113" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-838f58f2-22d1-5628-b984-cb6bd81d4f74" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d26afe6-a3f8-5d84-83c8-1468c5a521e7" "answer-f723a1bb-4bd1-5093-8a09-931299bce976" "answer-d08576a1-0a16-5570-9a0f-9b630cd00e74" "answer-0b365dcf-dfb8-5ede-acf7-bff7804315f6" "answer-838f58f2-22d1-5628-b984-cb6bd81d4f74"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d26afe6-a3f8-5d84-83c8-1468c5a521e7" :answer/id #uuid "7d26afe6-a3f8-5d84-83c8-1468c5a521e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-125\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f723a1bb-4bd1-5093-8a09-931299bce976" :answer/id #uuid "f723a1bb-4bd1-5093-8a09-931299bce976" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-625\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d08576a1-0a16-5570-9a0f-9b630cd00e74" :answer/id #uuid "d08576a1-0a16-5570-9a0f-9b630cd00e74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-625\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b365dcf-dfb8-5ede-acf7-bff7804315f6" :answer/id #uuid "0b365dcf-dfb8-5ede-acf7-bff7804315f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=125\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-838f58f2-22d1-5628-b984-cb6bd81d4f74" :answer/id #uuid "838f58f2-22d1-5628-b984-cb6bd81d4f74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=625\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cb4d13f6-05f0-58f1-8216-e5e0e82521cb" :question/id #uuid "cb4d13f6-05f0-58f1-8216-e5e0e82521cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-265843" :question/problem "If the sixth term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{16}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{4},$ then the third term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $6$th term is ${a}_{6}=16,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{6-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{4}^{2}} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{{4}^{5}}{{4}^{2}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=64,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={4}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third term  is ${a}_{3}=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-821f6ff9-8b02-5321-9f6c-8231f646103b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-821f6ff9-8b02-5321-9f6c-8231f646103b" :answer-field/id #uuid "821f6ff9-8b02-5321-9f6c-8231f646103b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f7fc47b7-94cb-597e-9fb8-34e73e5166b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f7fc47b7-94cb-597e-9fb8-34e73e5166b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7fc47b7-94cb-597e-9fb8-34e73e5166b1" :answer/id #uuid "f7fc47b7-94cb-597e-9fb8-34e73e5166b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b5f78cff-3c2d-5ff5-b715-c8fb25e53460" :question/id #uuid "b5f78cff-3c2d-5ff5-b715-c8fb25e53460" :question/math-academy-id "q-109828" :question/problem "Given that the $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $-125$, and the common ratio is $5,$ find the formula for the $n$th term." :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=5,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=-125,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{5-1} \\\\ -125 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{4} \\\\ -{5}^{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{5}^{n-1}}{5} \\\\  & =-{5}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0d82d16e-44b2-54a6-8e45-2ab848923d07"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d82d16e-44b2-54a6-8e45-2ab848923d07" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d82d16e-44b2-54a6-8e45-2ab848923d07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fce13607-7057-5951-b22c-0a53487566f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1d9acfbd-b37d-5bf8-8563-021375b3f0ac" "answer-71563d96-72fd-5a7a-873a-b97ad8340ba8" "answer-eec28517-10af-546d-b87d-a5fb95584b92" "answer-0a702943-5d20-5d78-b4eb-5b9620b8469b" "answer-fce13607-7057-5951-b22c-0a53487566f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d9acfbd-b37d-5bf8-8563-021375b3f0ac" :answer/id #uuid "1d9acfbd-b37d-5bf8-8563-021375b3f0ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-(-5)^{n-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71563d96-72fd-5a7a-873a-b97ad8340ba8" :answer/id #uuid "71563d96-72fd-5a7a-873a-b97ad8340ba8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eec28517-10af-546d-b87d-a5fb95584b92" :answer/id #uuid "eec28517-10af-546d-b87d-a5fb95584b92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a702943-5d20-5d78-b4eb-5b9620b8469b" :answer/id #uuid "0a702943-5d20-5d78-b4eb-5b9620b8469b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-{5}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fce13607-7057-5951-b22c-0a53487566f8" :answer/id #uuid "fce13607-7057-5951-b22c-0a53487566f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-{5}^{n-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079626 :tutorial/content "The general formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo illustrate how this works, suppose we want to find the $10$th term of the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n3, \\: 6, \\: 12, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nThe first term is $a_1=3,$ and the common ratio is $r=2,$ so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $10$th term, we can use this formula with $n={\\color{blue}10}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{10} & =3\\cdot {2}^{10-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{9} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 512 \\\\  & =1\\,536\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is $a_{10}=1\\,536.$ Using the formula was much quicker than writing out all $10$ terms of the sequence!" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078695 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "activity-cf46feab-8506-5fda-a24b-7fe0f9c9a46f" :activity/id #uuid "cf46feab-8506-5fda-a24b-7fe0f9c9a46f" :activity/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050955 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-10efb764-7a3b-560a-abec-d1eb97b24544" "step-1f1395c8-db59-556c-b73b-5656c71a970e" "step-04ef9537-6351-559a-927e-e766b414f4ca"] :activity/first-step "step-10efb764-7a3b-560a-abec-d1eb97b24544" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-10efb764-7a3b-560a-abec-d1eb97b24544" :step/id #uuid "10efb764-7a3b-560a-abec-d1eb97b24544" :step/content 17592186115696 :step/next "step-1f1395c8-db59-556c-b73b-5656c71a970e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1f1395c8-db59-556c-b73b-5656c71a970e" :step/id #uuid "1f1395c8-db59-556c-b73b-5656c71a970e" :step/content 17592186299577 :step/next "step-04ef9537-6351-559a-927e-e766b414f4ca" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-04ef9537-6351-559a-927e-e766b414f4ca" :step/id #uuid "04ef9537-6351-559a-927e-e766b414f4ca" :step/content 17592186299579 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186345274 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-fc8d5ef6-ad4d-51c7-8822-84767360e2eb" :activity/id #uuid "fc8d5ef6-ad4d-51c7-8822-84767360e2eb" :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049346 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-bd8e2704-5f40-5faf-b9f0-5291baf681f3" "step-271a47d9-c574-51a4-b4e3-461cef8bc255" "step-def0e380-3b81-5346-998d-cfdab0e48044" "step-b0747b36-5013-549d-a28a-aa3dd9ab1385" "step-5b83a2d9-8217-5e31-a5fa-035a5c1103a2"] :activity/first-step "step-bd8e2704-5f40-5faf-b9f0-5291baf681f3" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-bd8e2704-5f40-5faf-b9f0-5291baf681f3" :step/id #uuid "bd8e2704-5f40-5faf-b9f0-5291baf681f3" :step/content 17592186326775 :step/next "step-271a47d9-c574-51a4-b4e3-461cef8bc255" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-271a47d9-c574-51a4-b4e3-461cef8bc255" :step/id #uuid "271a47d9-c574-51a4-b4e3-461cef8bc255" :step/content 17592186345274 :step/next "step-def0e380-3b81-5346-998d-cfdab0e48044" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-def0e380-3b81-5346-998d-cfdab0e48044" :step/id #uuid "def0e380-3b81-5346-998d-cfdab0e48044" :step/content 17592186424620 :step/next "step-b0747b36-5013-549d-a28a-aa3dd9ab1385" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b0747b36-5013-549d-a28a-aa3dd9ab1385" :step/id #uuid "b0747b36-5013-549d-a28a-aa3dd9ab1385" :step/content 17592186345283 :step/next "step-5b83a2d9-8217-5e31-a5fa-035a5c1103a2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5b83a2d9-8217-5e31-a5fa-035a5c1103a2" :step/id #uuid "5b83a2d9-8217-5e31-a5fa-035a5c1103a2" :step/content 17592186345284 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186189892 :answer-field/choices 17592186189893] [:db/retract 17592186189892 :answer-field/choices 17592186189894] [:db/retract 17592186189892 :answer-field/choices 17592186189895] [:db/retract 17592186189892 :answer-field/choices 17592186189896] [:db/retract 17592186189892 :answer-field/choices 17592186189897] [:db/retract 17592186189964 :answer-field/choices 17592186189968] [:db/retract 17592186189964 :answer-field/choices 17592186189969] [:db/retract 17592186189964 :answer-field/choices 17592186189965] [:db/retract 17592186189964 :answer-field/choices 17592186189966] [:db/retract 17592186189964 :answer-field/choices 17592186189967] {:db/id 17592186192697 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f2fd8190-fb82-5675-b05f-057d697af7cd" "question-1883917d-29b4-5836-94bf-f6ba9fe053f5" "question-f867e6a6-dbb2-59f4-99c3-23f6d9793b91" "question-11b34dc5-01d5-59cc-afad-bce1d288e367" "question-aa587d06-3d76-54dc-bc14-c7a25e414d3a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192402 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y} & =1-x \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =3(1-x) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(3(1-x))}^{2} \\\\ y & =9(1-x)^{2} \\\\ y & =9(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = 9(x - 1)^2.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f(x) \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\leq 1, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192707 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f93430f2-f761-5264-8e7c-28207f8a2873" "question-3c4a5911-5181-51eb-9128-55f7bf7227a5" "question-0b14029b-c258-5cb1-9467-400506e4dea8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192415 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function ![](images/b9/b9a5eece426fa2aa66aac43b9afaf2812dcf483795a84d5e96fa89c0dcd71db6.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To find ![](images/6e/6e9ea2da83f324dd20158e4b08fb1c6968b6387d458964f21ed03648be954752.svg) we start by replacing ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) with ![](images/02/028bcadf67e469d96df97e89362f88bb8edca3ddea5579906987cc124663fba8.svg)\n![](images/d4/d4f5098eaa3eb85b97ec9547373f138922d22d734018ede816930c665248c0d5.svg)\n\nNow, interchanging ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) gives\n![](images/35/35c51b7dff42eeb3c3b2033c78495c07294048ba02628e583382c0807e43d192.svg)\n\nThen, we make ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) the subject of the equation, as follows:\n![](images/18/183e560a55c159a77b1ceacec766572e2279ad4e552242d638a9fdcf96f2c859.svg)\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n![](images/b5/b55f51d46d8917e5a44e4dae697c71a004580924cecd5a5f079e5eac7f480b3a.svg)\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) are\n![](images/f2/f2f23ba499fcbb1dee1964f08453be99bdf5ed7ec11581b773845c4aa307b64a.svg)\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ![](images/90/90bf8beda1404c3f6e9d9d9565922945fb59df9b0488050e3d2b72a153addd3c.svg) are\n![](images/96/96d9c5432e8f14ac6d9bc1841e37772cad060e3d09065129f73161726aeeffd2.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192698 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-37674798-c6eb-521f-b1bd-86df515c51cc" "question-14c0067f-8b9b-586e-a9fe-ecef26e510a6" "question-286413cf-e05b-52a9-9010-9fd10625bdfe" "question-f36e5ef3-ccb2-5795-aca3-91dfb93e14f0" "question-7e395659-7f2e-5dda-a6c4-f51ac9defcd5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192403 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4x + 1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4x + 1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4y + 1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4y+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{4y+1} & =5x \\\\ {(\\sqrt{4y+1})}^{2} & ={(5x)}^{2} \\\\ 4y+1 & =25{x}^{2} \\\\ 4y & =25{x}^{2}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}(25{x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\left(25x^2 - 1\\right).\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx \\geq -\\dfrac14, \\qquad f(x) \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq -\\dfrac14.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192699 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5cb5bb60-d097-50b4-9ba5-bde88e86a4d3" "question-22e0c565-a427-5cb0-889a-a9c4e5867def" "question-635cbae5-da30-5f6f-818b-8857829587ba" "question-082ab1b4-7b0d-5301-97bc-d24a513a580b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192404 :question/problem "The function $f(x) = 3\\sqrt{x + k} - 2$ has an inverse function given by $f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x + 2)^2 + 3.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\sqrt{x+k} - 2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = 3\\sqrt{y + k} - 2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{y+k}-2 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{y+k} & =x+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{y+k} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+2) \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y+k})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}(x+2))}^{2} \\\\ y+k & =\\frac{1}{9}(x+2)^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{9}(x+2)^{2}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x + 2)^2 - k.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-k = 3 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad k = -3.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx \\geq 3, \\qquad f(x) \\geq -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq -2, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f2fd8190-fb82-5675-b05f-057d697af7cd" :question/id #uuid "f2fd8190-fb82-5675-b05f-057d697af7cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-270776" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}-10.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}-10\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{y}-10.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{y}-10 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{y} & =x+10 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+10) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}(x+10))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{4}(x+10)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{4}(x+10)^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0,\\,f(x)\\ge -10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge -10,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-87121302-8c37-5665-97c7-f22db0a986f9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-87121302-8c37-5665-97c7-f22db0a986f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "87121302-8c37-5665-97c7-f22db0a986f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8ca68a00-32fb-52b6-9611-af784188ac51" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8ca68a00-32fb-52b6-9611-af784188ac51"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ca68a00-32fb-52b6-9611-af784188ac51" :answer/id #uuid "8ca68a00-32fb-52b6-9611-af784188ac51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}(x+10)^2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1883917d-29b4-5836-94bf-f6ba9fe053f5" :question/id #uuid "1883917d-29b4-5836-94bf-f6ba9fe053f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-277054" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=5\\sqrt[3]{x}+3.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=5\\sqrt[3]{x}+3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=5\\sqrt[3]{y}+3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\sqrt[3]{y}+3 \\\\ 5\\sqrt[3]{y} & =x-3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x-3) \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{5}(x-3))}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{125}(x-3)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{125}(x-3)^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-77ca7a05-762c-5491-b3c2-14aaeb97dc53"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-77ca7a05-762c-5491-b3c2-14aaeb97dc53" :answer-field/id #uuid "77ca7a05-762c-5491-b3c2-14aaeb97dc53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-760022a2-75fb-5225-a136-0dd915af89e1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-760022a2-75fb-5225-a136-0dd915af89e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-760022a2-75fb-5225-a136-0dd915af89e1" :answer/id #uuid "760022a2-75fb-5225-a136-0dd915af89e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{125}(x-3)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f867e6a6-dbb2-59f4-99c3-23f6d9793b91" :question/id #uuid "f867e6a6-dbb2-59f4-99c3-23f6d9793b91" :question/math-academy-id "q-148737" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=4\\sqrt[3]{x}-7.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=4\\sqrt[3]{x}-7\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt[3]{y}-7.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt[3]{y}-7 \\\\ 4\\sqrt[3]{y} & =x+7 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+7) \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}(x+7))}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{64}(x+7)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{64}(x+7)^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e400004-8c3b-55eb-9c62-48d0e98c825c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e400004-8c3b-55eb-9c62-48d0e98c825c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e400004-8c3b-55eb-9c62-48d0e98c825c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0f5a02a6-edc8-579d-9f3e-8685852c250f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-41a67b62-1927-57c7-b8fd-09c8ea128d9c" "answer-2a68acc7-11c9-53ec-9edf-5225b6802e14" "answer-0f5a02a6-edc8-579d-9f3e-8685852c250f" "answer-b3dda357-db83-5268-bb16-701863b5d617" "answer-f16acb2b-4b5f-577f-b3ff-41c909981ff7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41a67b62-1927-57c7-b8fd-09c8ea128d9c" :answer/id #uuid "41a67b62-1927-57c7-b8fd-09c8ea128d9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=4(x+7)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a68acc7-11c9-53ec-9edf-5225b6802e14" :answer/id #uuid "2a68acc7-11c9-53ec-9edf-5225b6802e14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=64{x}^{3}+7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f5a02a6-edc8-579d-9f3e-8685852c250f" :answer/id #uuid "0f5a02a6-edc8-579d-9f3e-8685852c250f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{64}(x+7)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3dda357-db83-5268-bb16-701863b5d617" :answer/id #uuid "b3dda357-db83-5268-bb16-701863b5d617" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{7}(x+4)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f16acb2b-4b5f-577f-b3ff-41c909981ff7" :answer/id #uuid "f16acb2b-4b5f-577f-b3ff-41c909981ff7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{7}{x}^{3}+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-11b34dc5-01d5-59cc-afad-bce1d288e367" :question/id #uuid "11b34dc5-01d5-59cc-afad-bce1d288e367" :question/math-academy-id "q-148431" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}-4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x}-4\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{y}-4.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{y}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =x+4 \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & =(x+4)^{3} \\\\ y & =(x+4)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=(x+4)^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b24942f2-dc2d-5709-9d5c-fc4d35113cac"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b24942f2-dc2d-5709-9d5c-fc4d35113cac" :answer-field/id #uuid "b24942f2-dc2d-5709-9d5c-fc4d35113cac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8bca345f-39c9-5521-bcba-a7055750252e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1547820e-1c6e-5874-a693-dbe63b833497" "answer-8bca345f-39c9-5521-bcba-a7055750252e" "answer-3cab048d-9187-5ff9-8179-12253f7ec205" "answer-f1b611fe-9f99-5df6-87f7-dbc5e83677ac" "answer-eb79fd11-c42f-5ea1-80c3-eeb2d1329843"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1547820e-1c6e-5874-a693-dbe63b833497" :answer/id #uuid "1547820e-1c6e-5874-a693-dbe63b833497" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=(x+2)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bca345f-39c9-5521-bcba-a7055750252e" :answer/id #uuid "8bca345f-39c9-5521-bcba-a7055750252e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=(x+4)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cab048d-9187-5ff9-8179-12253f7ec205" :answer/id #uuid "3cab048d-9187-5ff9-8179-12253f7ec205" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=4{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1b611fe-9f99-5df6-87f7-dbc5e83677ac" :answer/id #uuid "f1b611fe-9f99-5df6-87f7-dbc5e83677ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={x}^{3}+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb79fd11-c42f-5ea1-80c3-eeb2d1329843" :answer/id #uuid "eb79fd11-c42f-5ea1-80c3-eeb2d1329843" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={x}^{3}-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-aa587d06-3d76-54dc-bc14-c7a25e414d3a" :question/id #uuid "aa587d06-3d76-54dc-bc14-c7a25e414d3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-277045" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=6-4\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=6-4\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=6-4\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6-4\\sqrt{y} \\\\ 4\\sqrt{y} & =6-x \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{4}(6-x) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}(6-x))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{16}(6-x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{16}(6-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0,\\,f(x)\\le 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\le 6,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7ff35a3-b189-5c5b-b194-1dd1bc180b94"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7ff35a3-b189-5c5b-b194-1dd1bc180b94" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ff35a3-b189-5c5b-b194-1dd1bc180b94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-70556f37-c5ed-583c-a04b-123cc8508968" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70556f37-c5ed-583c-a04b-123cc8508968"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70556f37-c5ed-583c-a04b-123cc8508968" :answer/id #uuid "70556f37-c5ed-583c-a04b-123cc8508968" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(x-6)^2}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193001 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}-1\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}-1.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}-1 \\\\ x+1 & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+1} \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{x+1})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>-1,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189892 :answer-field/correct "answer-a685ea85-b22a-51d2-9d33-2ac79331c9a6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f64ff466-0bca-57e1-8856-2a4a8172d8c4" "answer-b704f50d-5c35-5b3e-a0c8-af0ac5fd5317" "answer-94ffbd69-746d-5e22-8c95-44e785cd24cf" "answer-a685ea85-b22a-51d2-9d33-2ac79331c9a6" "answer-00fd4c22-89d2-5fde-b119-9ce2163bb9d1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f64ff466-0bca-57e1-8856-2a4a8172d8c4" :answer/id #uuid "f64ff466-0bca-57e1-8856-2a4a8172d8c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=9(x+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b704f50d-5c35-5b3e-a0c8-af0ac5fd5317" :answer/id #uuid "b704f50d-5c35-5b3e-a0c8-af0ac5fd5317" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=9{x}^{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94ffbd69-746d-5e22-8c95-44e785cd24cf" :answer/id #uuid "94ffbd69-746d-5e22-8c95-44e785cd24cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}(x+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a685ea85-b22a-51d2-9d33-2ac79331c9a6" :answer/id #uuid "a685ea85-b22a-51d2-9d33-2ac79331c9a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00fd4c22-89d2-5fde-b119-9ce2163bb9d1" :answer/id #uuid "00fd4c22-89d2-5fde-b119-9ce2163bb9d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f93430f2-f761-5264-8e7c-28207f8a2873" :question/id #uuid "f93430f2-f761-5264-8e7c-28207f8a2873" :question/math-academy-id "q-277064" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{x}}+1.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{x}}+1\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{y}}+1.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{y}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{y}} & =x-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{2}{5(x-1)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{5(x-1)})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{25(x-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{25(x-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>1,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f2d41407-a63e-565f-bff1-4c6f38bb78e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f2d41407-a63e-565f-bff1-4c6f38bb78e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2d41407-a63e-565f-bff1-4c6f38bb78e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-13479a97-4e44-5471-9c23-134ec902cd16" :answer-field/choices ["answer-13479a97-4e44-5471-9c23-134ec902cd16"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13479a97-4e44-5471-9c23-134ec902cd16" :answer/id #uuid "13479a97-4e44-5471-9c23-134ec902cd16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25(x-1)^2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193013 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ x-3 & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ 3-x & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3-x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{y}}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{4}{3-x} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{4}{3-x})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{64}{(3-x)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{(3-x)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189964 :answer-field/correct "answer-fbf8be41-e92b-561f-ad73-49d08a3c31d5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b2900c9-1a52-5c45-aed2-605280ab5a10" "answer-8aaf3446-c684-5564-a8b6-8388976113a9" "answer-b0ed2b73-1ef1-577c-b9a7-c447148706e5" "answer-f085bb4e-fe8a-59a1-aa2f-9be46d61a226" "answer-fbf8be41-e92b-561f-ad73-49d08a3c31d5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b2900c9-1a52-5c45-aed2-605280ab5a10" :answer/id #uuid "7b2900c9-1a52-5c45-aed2-605280ab5a10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=27(x-4)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8aaf3446-c684-5564-a8b6-8388976113a9" :answer/id #uuid "8aaf3446-c684-5564-a8b6-8388976113a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{16}{(3-x)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0ed2b73-1ef1-577c-b9a7-c447148706e5" :answer/id #uuid "b0ed2b73-1ef1-577c-b9a7-c447148706e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{64}(3-x)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f085bb4e-fe8a-59a1-aa2f-9be46d61a226" :answer/id #uuid "f085bb4e-fe8a-59a1-aa2f-9be46d61a226" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{27}{(4-x)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbf8be41-e92b-561f-ad73-49d08a3c31d5" :answer/id #uuid "fbf8be41-e92b-561f-ad73-49d08a3c31d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{(3-x)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3c4a5911-5181-51eb-9128-55f7bf7227a5" :question/id #uuid "3c4a5911-5181-51eb-9128-55f7bf7227a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-277067" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{x}}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}} & =2-x \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{6(2-x)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{6(2-x)})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{36(2-x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{36(2-x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)<2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-40d99a89-ad22-5776-b72c-624e7b154468"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-40d99a89-ad22-5776-b72c-624e7b154468" :answer-field/id #uuid "40d99a89-ad22-5776-b72c-624e7b154468" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9e485cf9-61f6-51e5-b155-eeafd0535fb8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9e485cf9-61f6-51e5-b155-eeafd0535fb8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e485cf9-61f6-51e5-b155-eeafd0535fb8" :answer/id #uuid "9e485cf9-61f6-51e5-b155-eeafd0535fb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36(2-x)^2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0b14029b-c258-5cb1-9467-400506e4dea8" :question/id #uuid "0b14029b-c258-5cb1-9467-400506e4dea8" :question/math-academy-id "q-148747" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}+2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}}+2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{1}{4(x-2)} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4(x-2)})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{64(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{64(x-2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dcbe83e8-d72e-5bb6-b19b-e4a77cbd25ee"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dcbe83e8-d72e-5bb6-b19b-e4a77cbd25ee" :answer-field/id #uuid "dcbe83e8-d72e-5bb6-b19b-e4a77cbd25ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f5ceef0a-2fb9-54f4-a30f-c1f6dea6f8fa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bdf416e7-2557-519f-9c8d-e7755baf7e45" "answer-96b7e621-3bbb-55a8-a16a-7decc770db27" "answer-6518ddc4-2fcf-51ec-b05c-ac3df3011702" "answer-f5ceef0a-2fb9-54f4-a30f-c1f6dea6f8fa" "answer-52df6e02-287c-5a79-93aa-3df8e4867c19"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdf416e7-2557-519f-9c8d-e7755baf7e45" :answer/id #uuid "bdf416e7-2557-519f-9c8d-e7755baf7e45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=64(x-2)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96b7e621-3bbb-55a8-a16a-7decc770db27" :answer/id #uuid "96b7e621-3bbb-55a8-a16a-7decc770db27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=64{x}^{3}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6518ddc4-2fcf-51ec-b05c-ac3df3011702" :answer/id #uuid "6518ddc4-2fcf-51ec-b05c-ac3df3011702" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{3}}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5ceef0a-2fb9-54f4-a30f-c1f6dea6f8fa" :answer/id #uuid "f5ceef0a-2fb9-54f4-a30f-c1f6dea6f8fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{64(x-2)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52df6e02-287c-5a79-93aa-3df8e4867c19" :answer/id #uuid "52df6e02-287c-5a79-93aa-3df8e4867c19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-37674798-c6eb-521f-b1bd-86df515c51cc" :question/id #uuid "37674798-c6eb-521f-b1bd-86df515c51cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-148501" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt[3]{3y-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt[3]{3y-1} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{3y-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{3y-1})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{3} \\\\ 3y-1 & =\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3} \\\\ 3y & =\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{3}+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{3}+8).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-34761f03-b01e-5d22-8611-5c45286ea99b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-34761f03-b01e-5d22-8611-5c45286ea99b" :answer-field/id #uuid "34761f03-b01e-5d22-8611-5c45286ea99b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c68ea4c-d5d7-5c9b-a465-6fa09bd2ebf5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e37e5b73-e8d4-5af6-b8e6-580429037d3e" "answer-f2dd486e-0038-5ce9-9d90-476d55220192" "answer-6c68ea4c-d5d7-5c9b-a465-6fa09bd2ebf5" "answer-f02462fe-ef6d-54c3-9d42-6b2cb1464fdc" "answer-94cdd666-3a14-52b6-9bc6-23f645829972"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e37e5b73-e8d4-5af6-b8e6-580429037d3e" :answer/id #uuid "e37e5b73-e8d4-5af6-b8e6-580429037d3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=12(x-1)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2dd486e-0038-5ce9-9d90-476d55220192" :answer/id #uuid "f2dd486e-0038-5ce9-9d90-476d55220192" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=6({x}^{3}-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c68ea4c-d5d7-5c9b-a465-6fa09bd2ebf5" :answer/id #uuid "6c68ea4c-d5d7-5c9b-a465-6fa09bd2ebf5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{3}+8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f02462fe-ef6d-54c3-9d42-6b2cb1464fdc" :answer/id #uuid "f02462fe-ef6d-54c3-9d42-6b2cb1464fdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{(x+2)}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94cdd666-3a14-52b6-9bc6-23f645829972" :answer/id #uuid "94cdd666-3a14-52b6-9bc6-23f645829972" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{8}({x}^{3}+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-14c0067f-8b9b-586e-a9fe-ecef26e510a6" :question/id #uuid "14c0067f-8b9b-586e-a9fe-ecef26e510a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-277826" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-1}}+2.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-1}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-1}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-1}}+2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y-1} & =\\frac{3}{x-2} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{2y-1})^{3} & ={(\\frac{3}{x-2})}^{3} \\\\ 2y-1 & =\\frac{27}{(x-2)^{3}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{27}{(x-2)^{3}}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{27}{2(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{27}{2(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{1}{2},\\,f(x)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e843349-d8eb-5c3b-b2d7-adbfcd0cf240"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e843349-d8eb-5c3b-b2d7-adbfcd0cf240" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e843349-d8eb-5c3b-b2d7-adbfcd0cf240" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2dc46780-aad0-5339-8bcd-85933f0f6ae1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2dc46780-aad0-5339-8bcd-85933f0f6ae1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dc46780-aad0-5339-8bcd-85933f0f6ae1" :answer/id #uuid "2dc46780-aad0-5339-8bcd-85933f0f6ae1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-286413cf-e05b-52a9-9010-9fd10625bdfe" :question/id #uuid "286413cf-e05b-52a9-9010-9fd10625bdfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-148496" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x-3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y-3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y-3} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y-3} & =3x \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{y-3})}^{3} & ={(3x)}^{3} \\\\ y-3 & =27{x}^{3} \\\\ y & =27{x}^{3}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=27{x}^{3}+3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b9cdd843-64c4-50a3-ad0d-06cbfb60f172"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b9cdd843-64c4-50a3-ad0d-06cbfb60f172" :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cdd843-64c4-50a3-ad0d-06cbfb60f172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8c683e72-6d13-57a1-b930-019be2268271" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8532f603-d7b3-5d9b-a75f-906ea9009617" "answer-bace4cbf-a365-53e9-bce0-e3383e584bbb" "answer-8c683e72-6d13-57a1-b930-019be2268271" "answer-df3aa2c2-149a-55c8-b611-81c3367b402f" "answer-5fd39f34-1a8b-5f62-a888-d836f9832ac3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8532f603-d7b3-5d9b-a75f-906ea9009617" :answer/id #uuid "8532f603-d7b3-5d9b-a75f-906ea9009617" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=(x+3)^{3}-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bace4cbf-a365-53e9-bce0-e3383e584bbb" :answer/id #uuid "bace4cbf-a365-53e9-bce0-e3383e584bbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=(x-3)^{3}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c683e72-6d13-57a1-b930-019be2268271" :answer/id #uuid "8c683e72-6d13-57a1-b930-019be2268271" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=27{x}^{3}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df3aa2c2-149a-55c8-b611-81c3367b402f" :answer/id #uuid "df3aa2c2-149a-55c8-b611-81c3367b402f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=3{x}^{3}-27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fd39f34-1a8b-5f62-a888-d836f9832ac3" :answer/id #uuid "5fd39f34-1a8b-5f62-a888-d836f9832ac3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=9{x}^{3}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f36e5ef3-ccb2-5795-aca3-91dfb93e14f0" :question/id #uuid "f36e5ef3-ccb2-5795-aca3-91dfb93e14f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-148494" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x-1}+3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x-1}+3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{y-1}+3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{y-1}+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{y-1} & =x-3 \\\\ \\sqrt{y-1} & =2(x-3) \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y-1})}^{2} & ={(2(x-3))}^{2} \\\\ y-1 & =4(x-3)^{2} \\\\ y & =4(x-3)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=4(x-3)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1,\\,f(x)\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f4ff08e9-b72b-5342-b55b-78431f176928"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f4ff08e9-b72b-5342-b55b-78431f176928" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ff08e9-b72b-5342-b55b-78431f176928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dd9c5c09-10a6-5342-af9b-78b2b5ae6a65" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd9c5c09-10a6-5342-af9b-78b2b5ae6a65" "answer-fd71342d-0d39-5d72-ad19-e051b324e06a" "answer-480959df-c442-5539-928c-11f21e7bf1e3" "answer-2dcd6be4-7ab5-5b54-88c5-9399ecae152a" "answer-a85b8ce9-a004-5f51-988e-d3a07f8cb054"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd9c5c09-10a6-5342-af9b-78b2b5ae6a65" :answer/id #uuid "dd9c5c09-10a6-5342-af9b-78b2b5ae6a65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=4(x-3)^{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd71342d-0d39-5d72-ad19-e051b324e06a" :answer/id #uuid "fd71342d-0d39-5d72-ad19-e051b324e06a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=9(x-1)^{2}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-480959df-c442-5539-928c-11f21e7bf1e3" :answer/id #uuid "480959df-c442-5539-928c-11f21e7bf1e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=9(x-1)^{2}-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dcd6be4-7ab5-5b54-88c5-9399ecae152a" :answer/id #uuid "2dcd6be4-7ab5-5b54-88c5-9399ecae152a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x-3)^{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a85b8ce9-a004-5f51-988e-d3a07f8cb054" :answer/id #uuid "a85b8ce9-a004-5f51-988e-d3a07f8cb054" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)^{2}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e395659-7f2e-5dda-a6c4-f51ac9defcd5" :question/id #uuid "7e395659-7f2e-5dda-a6c4-f51ac9defcd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-148550" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ x-2 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ 2-x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{3y+2} & =\\frac{1}{2-x} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{3y+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2-x})}^{2} \\\\ 3y+2 & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ 3y & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>-\\frac{2}{3},\\,f(x)<2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ab8f4d5e-ee1c-54bc-8580-83267a345dee"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ab8f4d5e-ee1c-54bc-8580-83267a345dee" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab8f4d5e-ee1c-54bc-8580-83267a345dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f92861fe-09c8-581d-936f-0b83ad2bc2b4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2477bab7-122c-5324-8f02-3dc1b27b3327" "answer-72fb6298-18a6-56bf-a33e-72c4a66e7145" "answer-f92861fe-09c8-581d-936f-0b83ad2bc2b4" "answer-b3a95a87-c0da-5973-ba25-281d8d1b9a09" "answer-4d200f15-e854-5e0c-8e6d-204211621378"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2477bab7-122c-5324-8f02-3dc1b27b3327" :answer/id #uuid "2477bab7-122c-5324-8f02-3dc1b27b3327" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72fb6298-18a6-56bf-a33e-72c4a66e7145" :answer/id #uuid "72fb6298-18a6-56bf-a33e-72c4a66e7145" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f92861fe-09c8-581d-936f-0b83ad2bc2b4" :answer/id #uuid "f92861fe-09c8-581d-936f-0b83ad2bc2b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3a95a87-c0da-5973-ba25-281d8d1b9a09" :answer/id #uuid "b3a95a87-c0da-5973-ba25-281d8d1b9a09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{(x-2)^{2}}-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d200f15-e854-5e0c-8e6d-204211621378" :answer/id #uuid "4d200f15-e854-5e0c-8e6d-204211621378" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5cb5bb60-d097-50b4-9ba5-bde88e86a4d3" :question/id #uuid "5cb5bb60-d097-50b4-9ba5-bde88e86a4d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-277959" :question/problem "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6x-1}}+2,\\,x\\ne \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{81(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6x-1}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}} & =x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{6y-1} & =\\frac{2}{k(x-2)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{6y-1})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{k(x-2)})}^{3} \\\\ 6y-1 & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}} \\\\ 6y & =\\frac{8}{{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{81}\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e9e8e3bd-efc4-5161-8659-23fbd7d317a4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e9e8e3bd-efc4-5161-8659-23fbd7d317a4" :answer-field/id #uuid "e9e8e3bd-efc4-5161-8659-23fbd7d317a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8963ea18-7842-5daf-9fc8-8d25637a3833" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8963ea18-7842-5daf-9fc8-8d25637a3833"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8963ea18-7842-5daf-9fc8-8d25637a3833" :answer/id #uuid "8963ea18-7842-5daf-9fc8-8d25637a3833" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22e0c565-a427-5cb0-889a-a9c4e5867def" :question/id #uuid "22e0c565-a427-5cb0-889a-a9c4e5867def" :question/math-academy-id "q-277956" :question/problem "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8x+3}}+2,\\,x>-\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{2(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{3}{8},\\,x>2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$ Assume that $k>0.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8x+3}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}}+2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}} \\\\ \\sqrt{8y+3} & =\\frac{k}{x-2} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{8y+3})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{k}{x-2})}^{2} \\\\ 8y+3 & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ 8y & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{(x-2)^{2}}-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{3}{8},\\,x>2.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3eb0821a-73a6-5211-a52f-bc88da953dbc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3eb0821a-73a6-5211-a52f-bc88da953dbc" :answer-field/id #uuid "3eb0821a-73a6-5211-a52f-bc88da953dbc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7d73fe84-3859-5c8d-a8ac-924dd2eb2505" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d73fe84-3859-5c8d-a8ac-924dd2eb2505"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d73fe84-3859-5c8d-a8ac-924dd2eb2505" :answer/id #uuid "7d73fe84-3859-5c8d-a8ac-924dd2eb2505" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-635cbae5-da30-5f6f-818b-8857829587ba" :question/id #uuid "635cbae5-da30-5f6f-818b-8857829587ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-277840" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{x-5}+6$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=16(x-6)^{2}+5.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{x-5}+6\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5}+6.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5}+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5} & =x-6 \\\\ \\sqrt{y-5} & =k(x-6) \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y-5})}^{2} & ={(k(x-6))}^{2} \\\\ y-5 & ={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2} \\\\ y & ={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,k=4.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 5,\\,f(x)\\ge 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 6,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b8eacf56-5c06-55dd-aaa8-08c57c8e44fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b8eacf56-5c06-55dd-aaa8-08c57c8e44fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8eacf56-5c06-55dd-aaa8-08c57c8e44fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5a6bb1ec-ffed-5214-bafa-cf929f364451" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5a6bb1ec-ffed-5214-bafa-cf929f364451"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a6bb1ec-ffed-5214-bafa-cf929f364451" :answer/id #uuid "5a6bb1ec-ffed-5214-bafa-cf929f364451" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-082ab1b4-7b0d-5301-97bc-d24a513a580b" :question/id #uuid "082ab1b4-7b0d-5301-97bc-d24a513a580b" :question/math-academy-id "q-148570" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x+1}+k$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{3x+1}+k\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{3y+1}+k.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{3y+1}+k \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{3y+1} & =x-k \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{3y+1})}^{3} & ={(x-k)}^{3} \\\\ 3y+1 & =(x-k)^{3} \\\\ 3y & =(x-k)^{3}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-k)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(x-k)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-k=4\\,⟹\\,k=-4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5eae2b97-0cb3-5c07-814b-807db626bb1a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5eae2b97-0cb3-5c07-814b-807db626bb1a" :answer-field/id #uuid "5eae2b97-0cb3-5c07-814b-807db626bb1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-541dc755-cfc8-5462-825a-5222d3d5a3b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8cbf05be-0c0d-584b-8454-95280fcbd97e" "answer-541dc755-cfc8-5462-825a-5222d3d5a3b7" "answer-e4dd1f5a-912b-519e-afe1-3f55d58ca52b" "answer-27d37fb8-b5ab-549c-9f52-56163d362c4a" "answer-7d022d19-1586-599b-ba25-4ba70baadb54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cbf05be-0c0d-584b-8454-95280fcbd97e" :answer/id #uuid "8cbf05be-0c0d-584b-8454-95280fcbd97e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-541dc755-cfc8-5462-825a-5222d3d5a3b7" :answer/id #uuid "541dc755-cfc8-5462-825a-5222d3d5a3b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4dd1f5a-912b-519e-afe1-3f55d58ca52b" :answer/id #uuid "e4dd1f5a-912b-519e-afe1-3f55d58ca52b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27d37fb8-b5ab-549c-9f52-56163d362c4a" :answer/id #uuid "27d37fb8-b5ab-549c-9f52-56163d362c4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d022d19-1586-599b-ba25-4ba70baadb54" :answer/id #uuid "7d022d19-1586-599b-ba25-4ba70baadb54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193825 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll use our knowledge of solving radical equations to compute inverses of radical functions. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following radical function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\nTo compute the inverse of $f(x),$ we follow the usual four steps:\n\n**Step 1:** First, we replace $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we swap $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = 3\\sqrt{y} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Next, we solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{y}+2 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{y} & =x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}(x-2))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Finally, we replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x - 2)^2\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f(x) \\geq 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192863 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083161 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cce239c3-8163-5489-bdbb-7f61254813de" "question-f87f1803-048f-5856-842f-f5c9814dfee5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cce239c3-8163-5489-bdbb-7f61254813de" :question/id #uuid "cce239c3-8163-5489-bdbb-7f61254813de" :question/math-academy-id "q-35169" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(2,-1)$ lies on the graph $y=f(x),$ which point must lie on the graph of $y={f}^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Therefore, each point $(a,b)$ on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ (the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap).\n\nSince the point $(2,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ the point $(-1,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f9fdc229-414d-527d-9afc-4f509a46dc39"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f9fdc229-414d-527d-9afc-4f509a46dc39" :answer-field/id #uuid "f9fdc229-414d-527d-9afc-4f509a46dc39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ec0a0939-f953-5c16-bd75-6d602754382b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ec0a0939-f953-5c16-bd75-6d602754382b" "answer-6ac1f1cf-ecb0-509a-86f9-eca96f40ff42" "answer-9edbdb72-7744-54a7-ac70-88a496419317" "answer-1123f718-f438-501b-844b-142ecc377851" "answer-2a52576a-3d42-53b2-90ae-e2b56123d640"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec0a0939-f953-5c16-bd75-6d602754382b" :answer/id #uuid "ec0a0939-f953-5c16-bd75-6d602754382b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ac1f1cf-ecb0-509a-86f9-eca96f40ff42" :answer/id #uuid "6ac1f1cf-ecb0-509a-86f9-eca96f40ff42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9edbdb72-7744-54a7-ac70-88a496419317" :answer/id #uuid "9edbdb72-7744-54a7-ac70-88a496419317" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1123f718-f438-501b-844b-142ecc377851" :answer/id #uuid "1123f718-f438-501b-844b-142ecc377851" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a52576a-3d42-53b2-90ae-e2b56123d640" :answer/id #uuid "2a52576a-3d42-53b2-90ae-e2b56123d640" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083664 :question/problem "![](images/22/2280a258ec2cf548a5393772e497bece3ecbb61549c0d7260bad2146a9f3a094.png)\n\nThe graph of $f(x)$ is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of ${f}^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Of the given options, the only graph satisfying this condition is the one shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b2f729211c24879686601523f74935332dccbd6bf2e89b63af794b70dc721b1d.png)\n\nNotice that the points on $y=f(x)$ have corresponding points on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ with the coordinates swapped.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,3) & ⟶(3,1) \\\\ (\\sqrt{3},9) & ⟶(9,\\sqrt{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186080915 :answer-field/correct 17592186080918 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-f87f1803-048f-5856-842f-f5c9814dfee5" :question/id #uuid "f87f1803-048f-5856-842f-f5c9814dfee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-66618" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(-\\frac{3}{2},-3)$ lies on the graph $y=f(x),$ which point must lie on the graph of $y={f}^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Therefore, each point $(a,b)$ on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ (the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap).\n\nSince the point $(-\\frac{3}{2},-3)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ the point $(-3,-\\frac{3}{2})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1a4cf3dc-effc-5483-9e24-4a6b9e856a01"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1a4cf3dc-effc-5483-9e24-4a6b9e856a01" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a4cf3dc-effc-5483-9e24-4a6b9e856a01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c6ef62b6-0019-51f3-8c7e-c96d6e5b27b4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c6ef62b6-0019-51f3-8c7e-c96d6e5b27b4" "answer-0bf2d17b-61f8-5662-9b42-7ffe37131310" "answer-cc20a915-f28b-54bb-bb0d-d60458badfae" "answer-38ae4ee3-69b7-5477-99d6-69d092b66df5" "answer-dc172cf5-2436-5034-b7e9-eac317215744"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6ef62b6-0019-51f3-8c7e-c96d6e5b27b4" :answer/id #uuid "c6ef62b6-0019-51f3-8c7e-c96d6e5b27b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bf2d17b-61f8-5662-9b42-7ffe37131310" :answer/id #uuid "0bf2d17b-61f8-5662-9b42-7ffe37131310" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc20a915-f28b-54bb-bb0d-d60458badfae" :answer/id #uuid "cc20a915-f28b-54bb-bb0d-d60458badfae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38ae4ee3-69b7-5477-99d6-69d092b66df5" :answer/id #uuid "38ae4ee3-69b7-5477-99d6-69d092b66df5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc172cf5-2436-5034-b7e9-eac317215744" :answer/id #uuid "dc172cf5-2436-5034-b7e9-eac317215744" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-58d401b1-323f-56e8-8092-f299d6ef1cd2" :activity/id #uuid "58d401b1-323f-56e8-8092-f299d6ef1cd2" :activity/title "Graphs of Inverse Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048686 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-ae600ad6-5cdb-57a9-bb57-ebc5860ddaa6" "step-ceb432f0-d081-559d-bec1-57c991f96e15" "step-b0a29cb4-7b38-502f-99b6-f57e2ce5c1e6"] :activity/first-step "step-ae600ad6-5cdb-57a9-bb57-ebc5860ddaa6" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ae600ad6-5cdb-57a9-bb57-ebc5860ddaa6" :step/id #uuid "ae600ad6-5cdb-57a9-bb57-ebc5860ddaa6" :step/content "question-cce239c3-8163-5489-bdbb-7f61254813de" :step/next "step-ceb432f0-d081-559d-bec1-57c991f96e15" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ceb432f0-d081-559d-bec1-57c991f96e15" :step/id #uuid "ceb432f0-d081-559d-bec1-57c991f96e15" :step/content 17592186083664 :step/next "step-b0a29cb4-7b38-502f-99b6-f57e2ce5c1e6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b0a29cb4-7b38-502f-99b6-f57e2ce5c1e6" :step/id #uuid "b0a29cb4-7b38-502f-99b6-f57e2ce5c1e6" :step/content "question-f87f1803-048f-5856-842f-f5c9814dfee5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110662 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9cb24353-5541-5833-aaa7-2a27c3864cfb" "question-759e1619-eead-5907-8f58-8d6fbf13ba52" "question-af8fd3b0-1f7d-5ae6-bdf4-ff12a7c85e49"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9cb24353-5541-5833-aaa7-2a27c3864cfb" :question/id #uuid "9cb24353-5541-5833-aaa7-2a27c3864cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-424" :question/problem "${(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}})}^{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{(3y)^{3}}{{({x}^{2})}^{3}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power of product rule in the numerator and the power rule in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3y)^{3}}{{({x}^{2})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{3}{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2\\cdot 3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{27{y}^{3}}{{x}^{6}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-34e11030-4cbc-51ae-ad3e-605289271154"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-34e11030-4cbc-51ae-ad3e-605289271154" :answer-field/id #uuid "34e11030-4cbc-51ae-ad3e-605289271154" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cc14420e-7afe-5666-ae48-76b590082c86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d3af29e-c891-5b3d-91d7-0ee584444542" "answer-bd4ed26e-84c6-586f-98a0-82989e8a7dde" "answer-cc14420e-7afe-5666-ae48-76b590082c86" "answer-3010ad72-5935-55f4-a972-2133c2f9cacc" "answer-e785e421-4990-562e-bcc2-6b724b6ea31d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d3af29e-c891-5b3d-91d7-0ee584444542" :answer/id #uuid "8d3af29e-c891-5b3d-91d7-0ee584444542" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27{y}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd4ed26e-84c6-586f-98a0-82989e8a7dde" :answer/id #uuid "bd4ed26e-84c6-586f-98a0-82989e8a7dde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27{y}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc14420e-7afe-5666-ae48-76b590082c86" :answer/id #uuid "cc14420e-7afe-5666-ae48-76b590082c86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27{y}^{3}}{{x}^{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3010ad72-5935-55f4-a972-2133c2f9cacc" :answer/id #uuid "3010ad72-5935-55f4-a972-2133c2f9cacc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e785e421-4990-562e-bcc2-6b724b6ea31d" :answer/id #uuid "e785e421-4990-562e-bcc2-6b724b6ea31d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9{y}^{3}}{{x}^{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-759e1619-eead-5907-8f58-8d6fbf13ba52" :question/id #uuid "759e1619-eead-5907-8f58-8d6fbf13ba52" :question/math-academy-id "q-2648" :question/problem "If $x=\\frac{81{a}^{8}}{{b}^{2}{c}^{3}}$ and $y=\\frac{{b}^{7}{c}^{11}}{9{a}^{4}},$ then $xy=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute for $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =(\\frac{81{a}^{8}}{{b}^{2}{c}^{3}})\\cdot (\\frac{{b}^{7}{c}^{11}}{9{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{81{a}^{8}{b}^{7}{c}^{11}}{9{a}^{4}{b}^{2}{c}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the fraction, group the three variables separately, and apply the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{81{a}^{8}{b}^{7}{c}^{11}}{9{a}^{4}{b}^{2}{c}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{81}{9}\\cdot \\frac{{a}^{8}}{{a}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{{b}^{7}}{{b}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{c}^{11}}{{c}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{81}{9}\\cdot {a}^{(8-4)}\\cdot {b}^{(7-2)}\\cdot {c}^{(11-3)} & = \\\\ 9{a}^{4}{b}^{5}{c}^{8} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f0743b1d-d7b4-5ff8-9fc5-8af0138bd88a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f0743b1d-d7b4-5ff8-9fc5-8af0138bd88a" :answer-field/id #uuid "f0743b1d-d7b4-5ff8-9fc5-8af0138bd88a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-16185a8f-1aed-53a4-8147-fb86611335ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16185a8f-1aed-53a4-8147-fb86611335ef" "answer-28dcf52a-fdfa-5969-9364-91741721e814" "answer-8eb267c2-4d81-510b-a0ac-9f51187c8d5c" "answer-1ca2d046-b650-55fd-a1d2-c96295731924" "answer-c5129a7e-e66a-5aca-be73-9cc77c8b5f12"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16185a8f-1aed-53a4-8147-fb86611335ef" :answer/id #uuid "16185a8f-1aed-53a4-8147-fb86611335ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{a}^{4}{b}^{5}{c}^{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28dcf52a-fdfa-5969-9364-91741721e814" :answer/id #uuid "28dcf52a-fdfa-5969-9364-91741721e814" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{a}^{4}{b}^{8}{c}^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8eb267c2-4d81-510b-a0ac-9f51187c8d5c" :answer/id #uuid "8eb267c2-4d81-510b-a0ac-9f51187c8d5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{a}^{5}{b}^{4}{c}^{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ca2d046-b650-55fd-a1d2-c96295731924" :answer/id #uuid "1ca2d046-b650-55fd-a1d2-c96295731924" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{a}^{8}{b}^{5}{c}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5129a7e-e66a-5aca-be73-9cc77c8b5f12" :answer/id #uuid "c5129a7e-e66a-5aca-be73-9cc77c8b5f12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}^{4}{b}^{5}{c}^{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-af8fd3b0-1f7d-5ae6-bdf4-ff12a7c85e49" :question/id #uuid "af8fd3b0-1f7d-5ae6-bdf4-ff12a7c85e49" :question/math-academy-id "q-22308" :question/problem "If $\\frac{{y}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{13}}{{y}^{12}}={y}^{n},$ then $n=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by using the product rule in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{13}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2+13}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{15}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{15}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ {y}^{15+(-12)} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={y}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base ($y$), we can equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ 3 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $n=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6938548d-d593-5818-8e31-5db0d3b2686a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6938548d-d593-5818-8e31-5db0d3b2686a" :answer-field/id #uuid "6938548d-d593-5818-8e31-5db0d3b2686a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-91bde951-cb8f-577a-896b-ce42dc1b9fb7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-17eac886-4e63-5300-aa99-dd71380871fa" "answer-91bde951-cb8f-577a-896b-ce42dc1b9fb7" "answer-d8927f65-02e1-588d-9e78-6fe1b24c1d71" "answer-9b468f4f-c3de-5872-a718-b96e20c9f9a1" "answer-12c046f7-1d2c-5c08-94f0-b15b0633518f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17eac886-4e63-5300-aa99-dd71380871fa" :answer/id #uuid "17eac886-4e63-5300-aa99-dd71380871fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91bde951-cb8f-577a-896b-ce42dc1b9fb7" :answer/id #uuid "91bde951-cb8f-577a-896b-ce42dc1b9fb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8927f65-02e1-588d-9e78-6fe1b24c1d71" :answer/id #uuid "d8927f65-02e1-588d-9e78-6fe1b24c1d71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b468f4f-c3de-5872-a718-b96e20c9f9a1" :answer/id #uuid "9b468f4f-c3de-5872-a718-b96e20c9f9a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12c046f7-1d2c-5c08-94f0-b15b0633518f" :answer/id #uuid "12c046f7-1d2c-5c08-94f0-b15b0633518f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-635a428f-cdf1-5972-bb3d-42ad8fc88328" :activity/id #uuid "635a428f-cdf1-5972-bb3d-42ad8fc88328" :activity/title "Combining the Rules of Exponents With Algebraic Expressions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049252 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-3c44dfa5-7694-5da4-b5f0-5c65abf3a30f" "step-8b33002d-aae0-5d21-b694-3794848906a8" "step-385d5250-b237-518e-a161-9e072c92d0c6"] :activity/first-step "step-3c44dfa5-7694-5da4-b5f0-5c65abf3a30f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3c44dfa5-7694-5da4-b5f0-5c65abf3a30f" :step/id #uuid "3c44dfa5-7694-5da4-b5f0-5c65abf3a30f" :step/content "question-9cb24353-5541-5833-aaa7-2a27c3864cfb" :step/next "step-8b33002d-aae0-5d21-b694-3794848906a8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8b33002d-aae0-5d21-b694-3794848906a8" :step/id #uuid "8b33002d-aae0-5d21-b694-3794848906a8" :step/content "question-759e1619-eead-5907-8f58-8d6fbf13ba52" :step/next "step-385d5250-b237-518e-a161-9e072c92d0c6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-385d5250-b237-518e-a161-9e072c92d0c6" :step/id #uuid "385d5250-b237-518e-a161-9e072c92d0c6" :step/content "question-af8fd3b0-1f7d-5ae6-bdf4-ff12a7c85e49" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d27201ee-27d4-58dc-b2ff-368cd1c77509"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d27201ee-27d4-58dc-b2ff-368cd1c77509" :question/id #uuid "d27201ee-27d4-58dc-b2ff-368cd1c77509" :question/math-academy-id "q-140569" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $25{y}_{1}^{2}$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}-50(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1})+25(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+50{y}_{1}-25{y}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}_{1}^{2}+50{y}_{1}-25{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}+1-1-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}+1)^{2}-1-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & ={y}_{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+1, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & ={y}_{2}+1 \\\\ {z}_{1}^{2} & ={z}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${z}_{2}$-axis." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-178efdfc-1a0b-5ff3-b766-30d0f0cf8760"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-178efdfc-1a0b-5ff3-b766-30d0f0cf8760" :answer-field/id #uuid "178efdfc-1a0b-5ff3-b766-30d0f0cf8760" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7f2aabc6-1d15-561e-90e2-6684ee1deb5a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16a80f1c-1775-520a-8e07-b2d7e7c5b1b8" "answer-f58f9c99-39e6-5a04-b920-e4b11dd8