etract 17592186099339 :answer-field/choices 17592186099344] [:db/retract 17592186099339 :answer-field/choices 17592186099341] [:db/retract 17592186099339 :answer-field/choices 17592186099342] {:db/id 17592186100849 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6bd4c5ad-d5d4-5f1c-b1a3-32f3fc367847" "question-841b4d8f-31c4-51b2-96fd-34cf02a240b8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6bd4c5ad-d5d4-5f1c-b1a3-32f3fc367847" :question/id #uuid "6bd4c5ad-d5d4-5f1c-b1a3-32f3fc367847" :question/math-academy-id "q-26056" :question/problem "The point $Q$ with coordinates $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$ and the point $R$ with coordinates $(-1,-2)$ lies on the curve $y=g(x).$ If the curve $y=g(x)$ is related to $y=f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x)=f(x)+k$, then what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the point $R(-1,-2)$ lies $3$ units below the point $Q(-1,1).$ So, the graph of $y=g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by shifting it $3$ units down. \n\n![](images/7d/7d470919b374a5796b49ff81f893f37f5bb76afd4546a25459259f3b6339529b.png)\n\nAs a result, $k=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-559dfa33-1ae3-585f-bec7-2a590aa14d78"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-559dfa33-1ae3-585f-bec7-2a590aa14d78" :answer-field/id #uuid "559dfa33-1ae3-585f-bec7-2a590aa14d78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c3df13a-ef61-5c67-b5ac-9720cf6ce5b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-02e4c5e1-b54f-532d-b0ea-bdebad3f0d95" "answer-6c3df13a-ef61-5c67-b5ac-9720cf6ce5b1" "answer-d37c0952-f531-540d-b472-b096be136b1e" "answer-152655c8-8d0e-5d61-a322-4d75404f1f47" "answer-5fa7c064-ac96-5662-8162-cff29cbeaa27"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02e4c5e1-b54f-532d-b0ea-bdebad3f0d95" :answer/id #uuid "02e4c5e1-b54f-532d-b0ea-bdebad3f0d95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c3df13a-ef61-5c67-b5ac-9720cf6ce5b1" :answer/id #uuid "6c3df13a-ef61-5c67-b5ac-9720cf6ce5b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d37c0952-f531-540d-b472-b096be136b1e" :answer/id #uuid "d37c0952-f531-540d-b472-b096be136b1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-152655c8-8d0e-5d61-a322-4d75404f1f47" :answer/id #uuid "152655c8-8d0e-5d61-a322-4d75404f1f47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fa7c064-ac96-5662-8162-cff29cbeaa27" :answer/id #uuid "5fa7c064-ac96-5662-8162-cff29cbeaa27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101355 :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-3.$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)+1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+1$ is obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)-3$ by $4$ units *up.* \n\nHence, if the point $(2,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x)-3$, then the point \n\n$$\n(2,-1+4)=(2,3)\n$$\n\nlies on $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n![](images/46/468f1e720d1a4834f1547b0cd2fcb8c9bff1e0342d2d98e33a62bbf9d473701a.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(2,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x)-3$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)-3 & =-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-1+3 \\\\ f(2) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $x=2$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2)+1 \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(2,3)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186099339 :answer-field/correct "answer-28f9ac9c-0bcc-55e5-b0c1-5964a0642420" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca9d69c3-29e9-597f-b0dc-ddca64600d9c" "answer-8ce03ece-86f6-59a3-8602-b78e5b08064c" "answer-28f9ac9c-0bcc-55e5-b0c1-5964a0642420"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca9d69c3-29e9-597f-b0dc-ddca64600d9c" :answer/id #uuid "ca9d69c3-29e9-597f-b0dc-ddca64600d9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ce03ece-86f6-59a3-8602-b78e5b08064c" :answer/id #uuid "8ce03ece-86f6-59a3-8602-b78e5b08064c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28f9ac9c-0bcc-55e5-b0c1-5964a0642420" :answer/id #uuid "28f9ac9c-0bcc-55e5-b0c1-5964a0642420" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-841b4d8f-31c4-51b2-96fd-34cf02a240b8" :question/id #uuid "841b4d8f-31c4-51b2-96fd-34cf02a240b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86632" :question/problem "The point $Q$ with coordinates $(-2,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$ and the point $R$ with coordinates $(-2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=g(x).$ If the curve $y=g(x)$ is related to $y=f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x)=f(x)+k$, then what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the point $R(-2,-1)$ lies $2$ units below the point $Q(-2,1).$ So, the graph of $y=g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by shifting it $2$ units down. \n\n![](images/15/158488a446217332d0b70d2e6b116bc1e17e7e5b205ff5ebe4289a75ef05fa05.png)\n\nAs a result, $k=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e5978fb-3e4b-580d-a4fb-b9e5b65e85b5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e5978fb-3e4b-580d-a4fb-b9e5b65e85b5" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e5978fb-3e4b-580d-a4fb-b9e5b65e85b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-81da2310-6e25-5259-a85e-a5baa6d90882" :answer-field/choices ["answer-19683c15-6c13-53b8-8aea-5ea6854b2db1" "answer-81da2310-6e25-5259-a85e-a5baa6d90882" "answer-268c40f5-7adb-5466-bcd8-a03a2de00544" "answer-ef25ba64-af1d-535e-b7a0-c43c1199f31b" "answer-bacd3f0c-4868-59b8-9c7f-afe842c347b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19683c15-6c13-53b8-8aea-5ea6854b2db1" :answer/id #uuid "19683c15-6c13-53b8-8aea-5ea6854b2db1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81da2310-6e25-5259-a85e-a5baa6d90882" :answer/id #uuid "81da2310-6e25-5259-a85e-a5baa6d90882" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-268c40f5-7adb-5466-bcd8-a03a2de00544" :answer/id #uuid "268c40f5-7adb-5466-bcd8-a03a2de00544" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef25ba64-af1d-535e-b7a0-c43c1199f31b" :answer/id #uuid "ef25ba64-af1d-535e-b7a0-c43c1199f31b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bacd3f0c-4868-59b8-9c7f-afe842c347b4" :answer/id #uuid "bacd3f0c-4868-59b8-9c7f-afe842c347b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-87827238-a8af-5c97-9258-aa69642e3f16" :activity/id #uuid "87827238-a8af-5c97-9258-aa69642e3f16" :activity/title "Vertical Translations of Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050647 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-27c6e6f7-3931-5a76-88e4-d6e4ba6f3303" "step-01e04307-8907-58c2-83c8-12dc65028b26" "step-9f9b1109-0afb-53ad-9318-3e619c5a55d9"] :activity/first-step "step-27c6e6f7-3931-5a76-88e4-d6e4ba6f3303" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-27c6e6f7-3931-5a76-88e4-d6e4ba6f3303" :step/id #uuid "27c6e6f7-3931-5a76-88e4-d6e4ba6f3303" :step/content "question-6bd4c5ad-d5d4-5f1c-b1a3-32f3fc367847" :step/next "step-01e04307-8907-58c2-83c8-12dc65028b26" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-01e04307-8907-58c2-83c8-12dc65028b26" :step/id #uuid "01e04307-8907-58c2-83c8-12dc65028b26" :step/content 17592186101355 :step/next "step-9f9b1109-0afb-53ad-9318-3e619c5a55d9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9f9b1109-0afb-53ad-9318-3e619c5a55d9" :step/id #uuid "9f9b1109-0afb-53ad-9318-3e619c5a55d9" :step/content "question-841b4d8f-31c4-51b2-96fd-34cf02a240b8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125892 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the **global maximum** and **global minimum** of a function $f(x,y).$ Recall that the global maximum and minimum of a function are collectively known as **global extrema.** \n\nThe procedure we will describe for finding the global extrema is based on a general principle called the **extreme-value theorem,** which states the following:\n\n*Every function that is continuous on a closed, bounded region $D$ has global extrema (i.e., both a global maximum and a global minimum) on $D$*.\n\nLet's consider the function $f(x,y) =  e^{x^2- y^2}$ on the closed  region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2: \\,  x^2 +y^2  \\leq 1\\}.$ How do we find the global extrema?\n\nTo find the global extrema of a differentiable function on a closed, bounded region $D,$ we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1**:  Determine the critical points of $f$ in the interior of $D.$\n\n![](images/50/5057a4a96daa4819a70e733519c89802c28bde184183db2b4efbe69d3db36e56.png)\n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol f' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n![](images/b4/b48de1dcd3a240a1f1904dd290ef0665fc603c4cc5f99915915c6a12f79d74d9.svg)\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle \\cos{t}, \\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has three solutions, namely \n\n$$\nt =\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\: \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}2 \\in (0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval, namely, $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\:  \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ for each value of $t$, we obtain the following candidate points:\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\: (0,1), \\: (-1,0), \\: (0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nEvaluating $f$ at the five points we've found gives the following table of values:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(0,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $e$ | $1/e$ | $e$ | $1/e$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $\\dfrac1e$ at the points $(0,1), \\, (0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $e$ at the points $(1,0), \\, (-1,0).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]}]
